"BOGUS\": `event.type !== \"CREATED\"` → throw Error(\"first event must be CREATED...\"). But the spec says malformed events reject with TypeError. Hmm. So an unsupported type should be TypeError regardless of state.\n\nLet me reorder: validate the type first (in reduceEvent or before), throwing TypeError for unsupported types. Then do version/first-event checks.\n\nActually, let me validate type up front in the task:\n\n```js\nasync function task() {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  const supported = [\"CREATED\", \"AMENDED\", \"CANCELLED\"];\n  if (!supported.includes(event.type)) {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${event.type}`);\n  }\n  const current = ...;\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nBut then reduceEvent also throws for unsupported — redundant but fine. Let me keep type check in task up front to ensure TypeError for malformed type even on first event.\n\nHmm, but what about a version gap with a supported type — that's an Error not TypeError. Good.\n\nNow what about first event being AMENDED (supported type) — that's \"invalid first event\" → reject. With what error? Not malformed type. I'll use Error. Fine.\n\nLet me also think: \"malformed events reject with TypeError\" — does a version gap count as malformed? No. A version gap is an ordering issue. Error. Good.\n\nNow the per-trade queue. Let me store queues in a Map keyed by tradeId.\n\n```js\nconst states = new Map();\nconst queues = new Map();\n\nfunction project(event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    // handle in task instead? Let it throw synchronously? \n  }\n  const tradeId = event?.tradeId;\n  const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n  const run = prev.then(task, task);\n  const stored = run.then(noop, noop);\n  queues.set(tradeId, stored);\n  return run;\n}\n```\n\nWait, `task` is async and uses `event` from closure. But I need a fresh task per call. Let me define task inside project:\n\n```js\nfunction project(event) {\n  const tradeId = event && event.tradeId;\n  const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n  const run = prev.then(async () => {\n    // task body referencing event\n  }, async () => {\n    // same task body\n  });\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThat duplicates. Better: define a function and pass to both. Or use a helper:\n\n```js\nfunction project(event) {\n  const tradeId = event && event.tradeId;\n  const task = () => processEvent(event);\n  const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n  const run = prev.then(task, task);\n  queues.set(tradeId, run.then(() => {}, () => {}));\n  return run;\n}\n```\n\nWhere processEvent is an async function. Good. The `prev.then(task, task)` ensures task runs whether prev resolved or rejected (so a prior failure doesn't block). And `run.then(()=>{}, ()=>{})` stores a always-resolved promise so the chain continues. The returned `run` rejects appropriately for the caller.\n\nWait, but there's a subtle issue: if task itself rejects, `run` rejects, caller sees it. `queues.set` stores resolved. Next project: prev = resolved. prev.then(task) runs task. Good, not poisoned.\n\nNow about concurrency between trades: trade A's queue and trade B's queue are separate. If project(A) is awaiting applyToStore for A, project(B) runs its task immediately. Concurrent. Good.\n\nBut the test \"serializes concurrent events for one trade\" — within one trade, serial. Good.\n\nNow \"Inputs, callback values, returned states, and snapshots must not expose mutable internal state.\" I use copyState (structuredClone) for snapshot, returned state, and the state passed to applyToStore. And structuredClone(event) for the event passed to applyToStore. The `event` input itself — I don't mutate it. Good.\n\nBut wait: in processEvent, `states.set(event.tradeId, next)` stores `next` which is the reduced object. Is `next` derived from `previous` (the internal state) via spread? `{...previous, ...payload, ...}`. That creates a new object, so the stored `next` is a fresh object not sharing the old reference. But it does share primitive values (fine). It doesn't share arrays/objects because state only has primitives. Actually notional is a number, owner string, etc. Fine. But to be safe, should I clone payload values? payload.notional is a number. Fine. No nested objects in state. Good.\n\nBut one concern: `next` is stored, and I return `copyState(next)` (a clone). And getSnapshot returns clone. And applyToStore gets clone. So the stored `next` is never exposed directly. But could applyToStore mutate the passed state and affect... no, it's a clone. Good.\n\nHmm, but actually there's subtlety: when I do `await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClone(event))` BEFORE `states.set`, if applyToStore is slow, another event for same trade is queued behind. Good. The state only commits after. Good.\n\nNow, the `reduceEvent` for AMENDED uses `{...previous, ...payload}`. previous is the internal stored state object. Spreading it into a new object is fine (shallow). Then I override status and tradeId. Good.\n\nNow let me also double check: the second committed test expects after first failed project, getSnapshot null, then second project returns version 1. With my queue: first project enqueues task1. task1: current null, CREATED v1, reduce → next, applyToStore throws → task1 rejects, state NOT set. queues.set(stored resolved). Second project: prev = resolved. task2: current null (still), CREATED v1, reduce → next, applyToStore succeeds, states.set, return clone {version 1}. Good.\n\nBut wait — there's a timing subtlety. `await assert.rejects(projector.project(created()), ...)`. This awaits the first project's rejection. Then `assert.equal(getSnapshot, null)`. Then `await projector.project(created())`. Since the first already settled (rejected, and stored chain resolved), second runs fresh. Good.\n\nNow what about `fail` flag — applyToStore checks fail, throws first time, succeeds second. Good.\n\nLet me also make sure the `structuredClone` of event works — event is a plain object with primitives. Fine.\n\nNow, edge: what if applyToStore is synchronous (returns non-promise)? `await applyToStore(...)` handles it. Fine. In test, applyToStore is `async (_tradeId, state) => { await Promise.resolve(); persisted.push(state.version); }`. Fine.\n\nNow let me also handle: should project validate that event has tradeId? If event.tradeId is undefined, queues.get(undefined). It'd group all undefined-tradeId events. Probably fine; not tested. I'll not over-engineer.\n\nLet me also reconsider the \"malformed events reject with TypeError\" — I should make sure unsupported type throws TypeError. I'll put the type check at the top of processEvent.\n\nBut wait — what about duplicate/stale with unsupported type? E.g., trade exists, event with type \"BOGUS\" version 1 (stale). Should it return snapshot (stale) or throw TypeError (malformed)? Hmm. Malformed should probably throw TypeError regardless. Let me check type first, before version checks. So even a stale malformed event throws TypeError. That seems right — malformed is malformed.\n\nActually, reconsider: a duplicate event (same version) of a supported type returns snapshot. A duplicate of unsupported type... is it duplicate or malformed? I'd say malformed (TypeError) takes precedence. Check type first. Good.\n\nNow let me write the code.\n\nLet me also think about whether reduceEvent should be called for the gap/first-event cases. No — reduceEvent is only called when we actually proceed to persist. For rejects (gap, invalid first, terminal), we throw before reduceEvent. For duplicate/stale, return snapshot before reduceEvent. Good.\n\nWait, for \"invalid first event\" — first event is AMENDED (supported type) version 1. current null. Type check passes (AMENDED supported). Then current null → `event.type !== \"CREATED\"` → throw Error. Good. Not TypeError, but Error. Is that ok? The spec: \"A version gap or an invalid first event rejects without changing state.\" Doesn't specify error type. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually, should I reconsider whether field-validation errors on CREATED (e.g., notional = -5) should be TypeError or Error? The spec groups them under \"CREATED requires positive finite notional and non-empty owner\" and separately \"malformed events reject with TypeError\". I'll treat field violations as TypeError (malformed event). reduceEvent throws TypeError. Good. But this happens during reduceEvent which is called AFTER version checks pass, BEFORE applyToStore. So a CREATED with bad notional: current null, type CREATED, version 1, reduceEvent throws TypeError → task rejects, no state change, no persist. Good. \"rejects without changing state.\"\n\nNow, one more: the order of reduce vs applyToStore. I compute next (which validates), then applyToStore, then commit. If reduceEvent throws (bad fields), applyToStore not called, state not committed. Good.\n\nAlright, let me also think about the AMENDED where payload contains `status`. I override status = previous.status after spread. So payload.status ignored. Is that correct? \"AMENDED may replace either field\" — either field = notional or owner. So status shouldn't be replaceable via AMENDED. Good, I preserve it. Similarly tradeId preserved.\n\nBut what if payload contains version? I spread `{...previous, ...payload, version: event.version}` — version is set after payload spread, so event.version wins. Good. But if payload has version, it's overridden by event.version. Fine.\n\nNow let me write it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the structuredClone usage for performance — it's fine for tests.\n\nLet me also reconsider: \"Inputs ... must not expose mutable internal state.\" The input event — I pass structuredClone(event) to applyToStore. But I also read event.tradeId, event.type, etc. directly. I don't mutate event. Good. But do I expose internal state via the input? No. Good.\n\nOne more: getSnapshot returns copyState (clone) or null. Good.\n\nNow write the file.\n\nLet me write event-projector.mjs:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand both bugs:\n1. **Global queue** (`pending = pending.then(...)`) serializes all trades together.\n2. **State commits before persistence** (`states.set` runs before `await applyToStore`), and a rejection breaks the shared chain (poisoning).\n\nLet me write the repair."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d090642e03814d62841897f6","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"function copyState(state) {\n  return state ? structuredClone(state) : null;\n}\n\nfunction isFinitePositive(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0;\n}\n\nfunction isNonEmptyString(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"string\" && value.length > 0;\n}\n\nfunction validateStateShape(state) {\n  if (!isFinitePositive(state.notional)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"notional must be a positive finite number\");\n  }\n  if (!isNonEmptyString(state.owner)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"owner must be a non-empty string\");\n  }\n  return state;\n}\n\nconst SUPPORTED_TYPES = new Set([\"CREATED\", \"AMENDED\", \"CANCELLED\"]);\n\n// Pure reduction from the committed `previous` state to a candidate `next`\n// state for `event`. Throws TypeError for any malformed event or for an event\n// that would leave an invalid/incomplete state. Does not touch external state.\nfunction reduceEvent(previous, event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  const { type } = event;\n  if (!SUPPORTED_TYPES.has(type)) {\n    throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${type}`);\n  }\n  const payload =\n    event.payload && typeof event.payload === \"object\" ? event.payload : {};\n\n  if (type === \"CREATED\") {\n    if (previous) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"CREATED event for an existing trade\");\n    }\n    const next = {\n      tradeId: event.tradeId,\n      version: event.version,\n      status: \"active\",\n      notional: payload.notional,\n      owner: payload.owner,\n    };\n    return validateStateShape(next);\n  }\n  if (type === \"AMENDED\") {\n    if (!previous) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"AMENDED event without prior state\");\n    }\n    const next = {\n      ...previous,\n      ...payload,\n      version: event.version,\n      status: previous.status,\n      tradeId: previous.tradeId,\n    };\n    return validateStateShape(next);\n  }\n  // CANCELLED\n  if (!previous) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"CANCELLED event without prior state\");\n  }\n  return {\n    tradeId: previous.tradeId,\n    version: event.version,\n    status: \"cancelled\",\n    notional: previous.notional,\n    owner: previous.owner,\n  };\n}\n\nexport function createEventProjector(applyToStore) {\n  if (typeof applyToStore !== \"function\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"applyToStore must be a function\");\n  }\n\n  const states = new Map(); // tradeId -> committed state\n  const queues = new Map(); // tradeId -> always-resolved chain for serialization\n\n  async function processEvent(event) {\n    if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n      throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n    }\n    if (!SUPPORTED_TYPES.has(event.type)) {\n      throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${event.type}`);\n    }\n\n    const current = states.get(event.tradeId) || null;\n    const currentVersion = current ? current.version : 0;\n\n    // First event for a trade must be CREATED at version 1.\n    if (!current) {\n      if (event.type !== \"CREATED\" || event.version !== 1) {\n        throw new Error(\"first event must be CREATED at version 1\");\n      }\n      const next = reduceEvent(null, event);\n      await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClone(event));\n      states.set(event.tradeId, next);\n      return copyState(next);\n    }\n\n    // Duplicate or stale version: return current snapshot, do not persist.\n    if (event.version <= currentVersion) {\n      return copyState(current);\n    }\n\n    // Terminal trade rejects any later new version.\n    if (current.status === \"cancelled\") {\n      throw new Error(\"trade is already cancelled (terminal)\");\n    }\n\n    // Version gap.\n    if (event.version !== currentVersion + 1) {\n      throw new Error(\n        `version gap: expected ${currentVersion + 1}, got ${event.version}`,\n      );\n    }\n\n    const next = reduceEvent(current, event);\n    await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClone(event));\n    states.set(event.tradeId, next);\n    return copyState(next);\n  }\n\n  function project(event) {\n    const tradeId = event && event.tradeId;\n    const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n    const run = prev.then(() => processEvent(event), () => processEvent(event));\n    // Keep the per-trade chain alive regardless of this event's outcome so a\n    // rejection never poisons later work for the same trade (or another).\n    queues.set(tradeId, run.then(\n      () => {},\n      () => {},\n    ));\n    return run;\n  }\n\n  function getSnapshot(tradeId) {\n    return copyState(states.get(tradeId));\n  }\n\n  return { project, getSnapshot };\n}\n","path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/event-projector.mjs"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":654,"output":7410,"cacheRead":3776,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":6257,"totalTokens":11840,"cost":{"input":0.0006317639999999999,"output":0.02249676,"cacheRead":0.0007295232,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0238580472}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787544348601,"responseId":"gen-1787544348-zTUgwWu8gAUvDrD586m4","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d090642e03814d62841897f6","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 4552 bytes to benchmark/fixtures/T10-