{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":242,"output":1098,"cacheRead":13376,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":14716,"cost":{"input":0.00023377199999999998,"output":0.003333528,"cacheRead":0.0025842432,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0061515432}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_49322480accc4f3e9456434b","name":"write","arguments":{"content":"# Event projector incident\n\nStatus: resolved\n\n## Impact\n\nThe synthetic trade event projector (`createEventProjector`) had two defects:\n\n1. A temporary `applyToStore` rejection committed the reduced state anyway and\n   then permanently broke the projector: every subsequent `project` call (for\n   the same trade *and* for unrelated trades) rejected with the original store\n   error, so work could not make progress.\n2. All events across all trades were funneled through one global promise chain.\n   A slow store call for trade A blocked trade B even though their states are\n   independent, defeating concurrent progress for different trades.\n\n## Root cause\n\nIn `event-projector.mjs` the projector kept a single module-level\n`pending = Promise.resolve()` chain and chained every event onto it:\n\n```js\npending = pending.then(async () => {\n  const next = reduceEvent(states.get(event.tradeId), event);\n  states.set(event.tradeId, next);          // (a) commits before persistence\n  await applyToStore(...);                   // (b) rejection rejects `pending`\n  return copyState(next);\n});\n```\n\n- (a) `states.set(...)` ran *before* `applyToStore` was awaited, so a store\n  failure still left the new state committed. The committed test asserted\n  `getSnapshot(\"trade-1\") === null` after a failed persist and failed here.\n- (b) Because `pending` was reassigned to the `.then(...)` chain, a rejection\n  in any event rejected the shared `pending` promise. The next `project` call\n  chained onto a rejected promise and rejected too, with no recovery path —\n  poisoning all later work.\n- The single global `pending` chain also meant events for different trades\n  could not overlap, so unrelated trades were serialized behind one queue.\n\n## Fix\n\n`event-projector.mjs` was rewritten to:\n\n- Keep a per-trade serialization queue (`queues: Map<tradeId, Promise>`). Each\n  trade's events run in call order, but different trades have independent\n  queues and can make progress concurrently.\n- Compute the candidate `next` state, `await applyToStore(...)`, and only\n  `states.set(...)` *after* persistence succeeds. A rejection leaves the\n  committed state untouched.\n- Store an always-resolved promise per trade\n  (`run.then(noop, noop)`), so a rejection on one event never rejects the\n  chain; the next event for that trade still runs. The caller still observes\n  the original rejection via the returned `run` promise.\n- Enforce the version/state contract before persistence: first event must be\n  `CREATED` at version 1; later accepted events must equal `current + 1`;\n  duplicate/stale versions (`<= current`) return the current snapshot without\n  persisting; a version gap rejects; a `CANCELLED` trade is terminal and\n  rejects later new versions.\n- Validate event shape and fields (`CREATED`/`AMENDED` must leave positive\n  finite `notional` and non-empty `owner`); unsupported types and invalid\n  fields reject with `TypeError`.\n- Never expose internal state: `getSnapshot`, returned states, and the\n  values passed to `applyToStore` are all `structuredClone` copies; the input\n  event is cloned before being handed to the callback.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` was replaced with six `node:test` cases:\n\n1. Different trades progress concurrently instead of sharing one queue\n   (trade A blocks in the store while trade B completes).\n2. Duplicate and stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\n3. Version gap and invalid first event reject without changing state.\n4. A cancelled trade is terminal and rejects later new versions (a duplicate of\n   the terminal event still returns the snapshot).\n5. Malformed events and invalid fields reject with `TypeError`, and failed\n   amendments do not advance the version.\n6. Returned states, snapshots, and callback values do not expose mutable\n   internal state (mutating them does not affect later reads).\n\nThe committed test file (`event-projector.test.mjs`) was not modified and still\npasses.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\n- `structuredClone` is used for defensive copying; it cannot clone values\n  containing non-cloneable data (functions, `Symbol`-keyed state, etc.). The\n  fixture's events/states are plain JSON-like objects, so this is not exercised,\n  but a caller passing such payloads would get a clone error rather than silent\n  sharing.\n- Version ordering is enforced per trade based on `event.version`; the projector\n  does not deduplicate by a separate event id, so a genuinely new event that\n  reuses a stale version number is treated as a duplicate and silently ignored\n  (by design, per the contract).\n- The projector does not validate `tradeId`/`version` types beyond what the\n  comparisons require; malformed non-numeric versions would produce comparison\n  behavior rather than a typed error.\n","path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md"}}}}
