ction in `pending.then` would break the whole chain. With per-trade queues, a rejection must not poison later work for that trade.\n\nLet me think about the requirements carefully:\n\n- Events for one trade applied serially in call order. Different trades progress concurrently.\n- First accepted event is CREATED at version 1. Later accepted events use current version + 1 exactly.\n- Duplicate or stale versions return current snapshot without persisting.\n- Version gap or invalid first event rejects without changing state.\n- CANCELLED trade is terminal; rejects later new version.\n- State commits only after applyToStore succeeds. Rejection must not poison later work for that trade or another trade.\n- CREATED requires positive finite notional, non-empty owner. AMENDED may replace either field but must leave valid complete state.\n- Supported types: CREATED, AMENDED, CANCELLED; malformed reject with TypeError.\n- Inputs, callback values, returned states, snapshots must not expose mutable internal state.\n\nLet me design:\n\n```js\nfunction copyState(state) {\n  return state ? structuredClone(state) : null;\n}\n\nfunction isFinitePositive(n) {\n  return typeof n === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(n) && n > 0;\n}\n\nfunction isValidState(state) {\n  return state && isFinitePositive(state.notional) && typeof state.owner === \"string\" && state.owner.length > 0;\n}\n\nfunction reduceEvent(previous, event) {\n  // validate event shape\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  // validate tradeId, version, type, payload?\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nLet me think about what counts as \"malformed events reject with TypeError\". Supported types are CREATED, AMENDED, CANCELLED. So an unknown type rejects with TypeError. Also probably missing/invalid fields.\n\nLet me be careful but not overly strict. Let me focus on the clear requirements.\n\nLet me design the projection logic per trade:\n\nFor a trade, we maintain:\n- `state`: current committed state (or null)\n- `queue`: promise chain for serialization\n\nWhen `project(event)` is called:\n- Enqueue a task for that trade's queue.\n- The task:\n  1. Validate event type is supported (else reject TypeError).\n  2. Compute current version: `state ? state.version : 0`. Expected next version = current + 1.\n  3. Check version rules:\n     - If state is null (no prior):\n       - Event must be CREATED with version 1. Otherwise reject (version gap / invalid first event).\n     - If state exists:\n       - If event.version <= state.version: duplicate or stale → return copyState(state) without persisting.\n       - If event.version === state.version + 1: proceed.\n       - If event.version > state.version + 1: version gap → reject.\n       - If state.status === \"cancelled\": terminal → reject a later new version. (Even version+1? \"A CANCELLED trade is terminal and rejects a later new version.\" So reject.)\n  4. Reduce event to compute next state (with validation for CREATED/AMENDED fields).\n  5. Validate the resulting state is valid complete.\n  6. Call applyToStore(tradeId, copyState(next), copyEvent(event)).\n  7. On success: commit state = next. Return copyState(next).\n  8. On rejection: do NOT commit. The rejection propagates but must not poison the queue.\n\nWait, for duplicate/stale returning current snapshot without persisting — that returns a resolved value (the snapshot), not a rejection. So it doesn't go through applyToStore.\n\nFor version gap / invalid first event / terminal rejection — these reject. But must not poison the queue. So the queue chain must catch and continue.\n\nLet me structure the queue so each task returns a promise; the next task runs regardless. Using:\n\n```js\nfunction enqueue(tradeId, task) {\n  const queues = ...;\n  const prev = queues.get(tradeId) || Promise.resolve();\n  const next = prev.then(task, task); // run task regardless of prev resolution\n  // but we want to keep the chain going even if task rejects\n  queues.set(tradeId, next.then(() => {}, () => {}));\n  return next;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but we want the returned promise to reject (so caller sees the rejection), but the stored chain should not be rejected so subsequent events still run. So:\n\n```js\nconst run = prev.then(task, task); // run regardless\nconst stored = run.then(() => {}, () => {}); // always resolved\nqueues.set(tradeId, stored);\nreturn run;\n```\n\nBut there's subtlety: if task throws synchronously (TypeError), `.then(task, task)` — task is async so it returns a promise; rejection handled. Actually task should be async.\n\nNow, \"Duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" — return value is the snapshot (resolved). Good.\n\n\"version gap or invalid first event rejects without changing state\" — rejection. Good.\n\nNow, ordering and concurrency: Each trade has its own queue. Different trades have separate queues → concurrent. Good.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtlety with the test \"serializes concurrent events for one trade\":\n```js\nconst first = projector.project(created());\nconst second = projector.project({version 2 AMENDED});\nawait Promise.all([first, second]);\nassert.deepEqual(persisted, [1, 2]);\n```\nThe applyToStore does `await Promise.resolve()` then pushes. With per-trade queue, first runs, persists version 1, then second runs persists version 2. Good. persisted = [1,2]. Good.\n\nNow the second test \"persistence rejection does not commit state or poison later work\":\n```js\nlet fail = true;\nconst projector = createEventProjector(async () => {\n  if (fail) { fail = false; throw new Error(\"temporary store failure\"); }\n});\nawait assert.rejects(projector.project(created()), /temporary store failure/);\nassert.equal(projector.getSnapshot(\"trade-1\"), null);\nassert.equal((await projector.project(created())).version, 1);\n```\n\nSo first project: state is null, event CREATED v1. Compute next state. Call applyToStore → throws. State NOT committed (still null). project rejects. Good.\ngetSnapshot → null. Good.\nSecond project: state still null, CREATED v1 again. Compute next, applyToStore succeeds (fail now false). Commit state = {version 1,...}. Return copy. version 1. Good.\n\nNow, the order: we must NOT commit before applyToStore. In my task, I compute `next` (a local), call applyToStore, then `states.set(tradeId, next)` only after success. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtle issue with serialization: if applyToStore for event 1 is still pending when event 2 is enqueued, event 2's task waits for event 1's task to settle. With per-trade queue that's correct.\n\nNow validation details:\n\nCREATED: requires positive finite notional and non-empty owner. The payload.notional and payload.owner. What if payload missing? Throw TypeError? Or just invalid. Let me think — \"CREATED requires positive finite notional and non-empty owner.\" If invalid, what happens? Probably reject. With TypeError? The spec says \"malformed events reject with TypeError\". Hmm, but invalid notional isn't really \"malformed type\". Let me consider what's safest.\n\nActually let me re-read: \"Supported types are CREATED, AMENDED, and CANCELLED; malformed events reject with TypeError.\" This is about event type. So unknown type → TypeError.\n\nFor CREATED with invalid notional/owner — \"must leave a valid complete state\" type rules. I think these should reject too. The question is whether TypeError or Error. Let me use a generic Error for field validation, and TypeError only for unsupported/missing type. Actually, to be safe, let me think about what tests might check.\n\nThe committed test only checks the two scenarios. The student tests I write. So I have freedom. But the task description says \"malformed events reject with TypeError\" — I'll interpret malformed = bad event type or non-object event. Field validation errors I'll throw as Error (or TypeError?).\n\nHmm. Let me think about \"an invalid first event rejects without changing state\" — invalid first event could be a non-CREATED first event, or CREATED with bad fields. Either way reject. I'll make field validation throw TypeError too to be consistent with \"malformed\". Actually, let me make a distinction: structural issues (type not supported, event not object) → TypeError. Field validation (bad notional, bad owner) → TypeError as well since they make the event malformed. Let me just use TypeError for all validation failures to be safe and consistent.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The duplicate/stale case returns the snapshot (resolves), doesn't reject. Version gap rejects. Should version gap be TypeError? No, that's a different kind of error. Let me use a plain Error for version issues, and TypeError for malformed events (bad type, bad fields). Hmm, but actually it doesn't hugely matter for the tests since I'm writing them.\n\nLet me just be reasonable:\n- Unsupported event type → TypeError\n- Event not an object / missing fields → TypeError\n- CREATED with invalid notional/owner → TypeError (malformed)\n- AMENDED resulting in invalid state → TypeError\n- Version gap → Error (not TypeError)\n- Invalid first event (non-CREATED when no state, or CREATED with wrong version) → Error\n- Terminal cancelled rejecting new version → Error\n\nHmm, but \"an invalid first event rejects\" — is that version-related or type-related? An invalid first event = first event isn't CREATED at v1. I'll use Error.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. Let me keep it simple and consistent. I'll make validation throw TypeError for malformed/unsupported, and throw Error for ordering/version violations. That seems cleanest.\n\nLet me now think about the version check carefully:\n\nCurrent version = state ? state.version : 0.\n- If state is null (first event ever for this trade):\n  - Must be CREATED with version 1. If not CREATED → reject (invalid first event). If version !== 1 → reject (invalid first event / gap).\n  - Actually: \"The first accepted event is CREATED at version 1.\" and \"A version gap or an invalid first event rejects without changing state.\" So first event must be CREATED v1.\n- If state exists:\n  - If state.status === \"cancelled\": terminal. Reject any new version. (Even duplicates? \"rejects a later new version\". A duplicate would have version <= current. Hmm. \"Duplicate or stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\" Does that apply to cancelled trades too? A duplicate of the CANCELLED event... Let me think. The cancelled state's version is, say, 3. A duplicate event with version 3 → stale, return snapshot. A new event version 4 → reject (terminal). I think duplicate/stale check should come first: if version <= current, return snapshot. Else if cancelled, reject. Else proceed.)\n\nWait, let me order the checks for an existing state:\n1. If event.version < state.version → stale → return snapshot (resolve).\n2. If event.version === state.version → duplicate → return snapshot (resolve).\n   (So version <= current → return snapshot.)\n3. If event.version > state.version + 1 → version gap → reject.\n4. If event.version === state.version + 1:\n   - If state.status === \"cancelled\" → terminal → reject.\n   - Else proceed: reduce, validate, persist, commit.\n\nThat handles all. Let me also consider: what about a duplicate CREATED when state already exists with that version? version === state.version → return snapshot. Good, doesn't re-create.\n\nNow the reduce + validation. Let me restructure reduceEvent to validate fields and produce next state, throwing TypeError on invalid.\n\n```js\nfunction reduceEvent(previous, event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  const { type, tradeId, version, payload } = event;\n  if (typeof type !== \"string\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event.type must be a string\");\n  }\n  // ... but tradeId/version validation?\n}\n```\n\nHmm, let me not over-validate event shape. Let me focus on what's required. Let me handle type and payload fields.\n\nActually for robustness and the \"must not expose mutable internal state\" + \"inputs ... must not expose mutable internal state\", I use structuredClone / copies everywhere. Good.\n\nLet me write reduceEvent:\n\n```js\nfunction isFinitePositive(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"number\" && Number.isFinite(value) && value > 0;\n}\n\nfunction isNonEmptyString(value) {\n  return typeof value === \"string\" && value.length > 0;\n}\n\nfunction validateStateShape(state) {\n  if (!isFinitePositive(state.notional)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"notional must be a positive finite number\");\n  }\n  if (!isNonEmptyString(state.owner)) {\n    throw new TypeError(\"owner must be a non-empty string\");\n  }\n  return state;\n}\n\nfunction reduceEvent(previous, event) {\n  if (event === null || typeof event !== \"object\") {\n    throw new TypeError(\"event must be an object\");\n  }\n  const { type } = event;\n  if (type === \"CREATED\") {\n    if (previous) {\n      // shouldn't happen if called correctly; but reduceEvent shouldn't be called for duplicates\n      throw new TypeError(\"CREATED event for existing trade\");\n    }\n    const payload = event.payload || {};\n    const next = {\n      tradeId: event.tradeId,\n      version: event.version,\n      status: \"active\",\n      notional: payload.notional,\n      owner: payload.owner,\n    };\n    return validateStateShape(next);\n  }\n  if (type === \"AMENDED\") {\n    if (!previous) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"AMENDED event without prior state\");\n    }\n    const payload = event.payload || {};\n    const next = {\n      ...previous,\n      ...payload,\n      version: event.version,\n    };\n    // ensure status not overwritten? payload could contain status. \"AMENDED may replace either field but must leave a valid complete state.\" Either field = notional or owner. So payload should only contain notional/owner. If payload has status... hmm. Let me preserve status from previous and only allow notional/owner changes.\n    next.status = previous.status;\n    next.tradeId = previous.tradeId;\n    return validateStateShape(next);\n  }\n  if (type === \"CANCELLED\") {\n    if (!previous) {\n      throw new TypeError(\"CANCELLED event without prior state\");\n    }\n    return {\n      tradeId: previous.tradeId,\n      version: event.version,\n      status: \"cancelled\",\n      notional: previous.notional,\n      owner: previous.owner,\n    };\n  }\n  throw new TypeError(`unsupported event type: ${type}`);\n}\n```\n\nWait — the test for AMENDED:\n```js\n{ type: \"AMENDED\", payload: { notional: 125 } }\n```\nexpects result:\n```js\n{ tradeId: \"trade-1\", version: 2, status: \"active\", notional: 125, owner: \"desk-a\" }\n```\nMy reduce: `{...previous (tradeId, version, status, notional, owner), ...payload (notional:125), version: 2}` then `next.status = previous.status (\"active\")`, `next.tradeId = previous.tradeId`. Result: {tradeId, version:2, status:\"active\", notional:125, owner:\"desk-a\"}. \n\nBut wait, order: `{...previous, ...payload, version}` — version from event overrides previous.version. Good. Then I set status and tradeId from previous (in case payload had them). Good.\n\nNow, AMENDED \"may replace either field but must leave a valid complete state.\" So if AMENDED payload sets notional to -5, validateStateShape throws TypeError. Good. If AMENDED payload omits owner, owner stays from previous. Good.\n\nNow the projection task:\n\n```js\nasync function task() {\n  const current = states.get(event.tradeId) || null;\n  const currentVersion = current ? current.version : 0;\n\n  // first event\n  if (!current) {\n    if (event.type !== \"CREATED\" || event.version !== 1) {\n      throw new Error(\"first event must be CREATED at version 1\");\n    }\n    const next = reduceEvent(null, event); // validates\n    await applyToStore(event.tradeId, copyState(next), structuredClo